1
00:00:00,310 --> 00:00:02,460
SpaceX wants to do this twice.

2
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:03,940
First, the booster.

3
00:00:04,149 --> 00:00:07,260
Then, the spacecraft—after
it comes back from orbit.

4
00:00:07,530 --> 00:00:09,220
And if that handoff goes wrong,

5
00:00:09,310 --> 00:00:11,560
the rocket isn't the only thing at risk.

6
00:00:13,750 --> 00:00:15,900
Super Heavy is the booster underneath.

7
00:00:16,130 --> 00:00:18,560
Starship is the pointed spacecraft on top.

8
00:00:18,710 --> 00:00:21,480
They launch together, then
take very different trips.

9
00:00:21,710 --> 00:00:24,800
The booster turns back toward the
coast while the ship keeps going.

10
00:00:25,190 --> 00:00:26,740
In October 2024,

11
00:00:26,870 --> 00:00:30,300
SpaceX caught a returning booster
with the tower's giant arms.

12
00:00:31,210 --> 00:00:35,480
Now, for Flight 15, it says it
wants to attempt both recoveries.

13
00:00:35,750 --> 00:00:37,420
Two catches during one mission,

14
00:00:37,510 --> 00:00:39,100
separated by the ship's flight.

15
00:00:39,470 --> 00:00:42,700
The new challenge is bringing a
spacecraft back through the atmosphere,

16
00:00:42,770 --> 00:00:46,260
turning it upright, and delivering it
to the machinery waiting on the ground.

17
00:00:46,650 --> 00:00:49,200
The launch site becomes
part of the landing system.

18
00:00:50,730 --> 00:00:52,600
Look closely at that first catch.

19
00:00:52,810 --> 00:00:55,580
The booster is still firing
its engines on the way in.

20
00:00:55,870 --> 00:00:59,350
Those engines remove almost all of its
downward speed before the arms take

21
00:00:59,390 --> 00:01:00,290
the load.

22
00:01:00,370 --> 00:01:03,440
The arms support dedicated features
near the top of the vehicle.

23
00:01:03,650 --> 00:01:06,300
They aren't crushing a steel
fuel tank until it sticks.

24
00:01:06,730 --> 00:01:09,420
Think of lowering a heavy
object onto two shelves:

25
00:01:09,650 --> 00:01:11,800
line up the supports, arrive gently,

26
00:01:11,990 --> 00:01:12,960
transfer the weight.

27
00:01:13,310 --> 00:01:14,260
Sounds reasonable.

28
00:01:14,980 --> 00:01:16,860
Except the object is a rocket,

29
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:20,330
the shelves move, and the crane
is the rocket's own engines.

30
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:23,070
Being between the arms is only the start.

31
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:25,210
Its motion has to be under control.

32
00:01:26,560 --> 00:01:29,090
The ship adds a completely
different problem:

33
00:01:29,380 --> 00:01:30,390
orbital speed.

34
00:01:30,820 --> 00:01:34,380
Getting to orbit means moving fast
enough sideways to keep falling around

35
00:01:34,420 --> 00:01:35,200
Earth.

36
00:01:35,240 --> 00:01:37,750
Coming home means getting
rid of that energy.

37
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:40,030
The atmosphere does much of the braking,

38
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:43,070
while the gas around the
vehicle becomes intensely hot.

39
00:01:43,380 --> 00:01:45,970
That's what the black tiled
side is protecting it from.

40
00:01:46,820 --> 00:01:49,010
The ship has to hold
the right orientation,

41
00:01:49,140 --> 00:01:50,990
keep its heat shield facing the flow,

42
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:53,850
and survive with its control
systems still working.

43
00:01:54,200 --> 00:01:57,650
A damaged spacecraft reaching the
right coastline is not enough.

44
00:01:57,860 --> 00:01:59,950
It still needs engines, control,

45
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:01,990
and a final maneuver beside a tower.

46
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:05,270
Surviving reentry only gets
it to the next problem.

47
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:07,580
Lower down,

48
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:09,650
Starship descends broadside,

49
00:02:09,720 --> 00:02:13,270
using its body against the air while
the flaps help control its attitude.

50
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:16,650
Then the engines relight and
swing the vehicle toward upright.

51
00:02:16,920 --> 00:02:20,730
SpaceX has already demonstrated this
flip and landing burn over the ocean.

52
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:22,270
Watch the change:

53
00:02:23,860 --> 00:02:25,740
falling sideways becomes a powered,

54
00:02:25,780 --> 00:02:27,290
nearly vertical descent.

55
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:30,090
The planned tower catch
adds a physical target.

56
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:31,860
The ship needs the right position,

57
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:35,250
orientation and motion when its
support features reach the arms.

58
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:38,930
A successful ocean landing proves
an important part of that sequence.

59
00:02:39,180 --> 00:02:41,110
The tower asks for something extra:

60
00:02:41,340 --> 00:02:44,770
put the attachment points where the
receiving surfaces will actually be.

61
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:49,270
Those receiving surfaces
belong to a moving machine.

62
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:52,930
The arms swing, and their carriage
travels up and down the tower.

63
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:55,190
Look at the deep trusses underneath:

64
00:02:55,940 --> 00:02:58,080
the load has to pass
through those members,

65
00:02:58,120 --> 00:03:00,040
into the carriage, down the tower,

66
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:01,210
and into the ground.

67
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:04,250
The newer tower uses shorter arms,

68
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:08,530
and the newer booster has three larger
grid fins with revised catch features.

69
00:03:08,840 --> 00:03:13,340
For a moment, flying machinery and ground
machinery have to become one supported

70
00:03:13,380 --> 00:03:14,280
structure.

71
00:03:14,340 --> 00:03:17,580
The spacecraft and the catcher must
complete their own movements in the

72
00:03:17,620 --> 00:03:19,850
right condition, at the right moment.

73
00:03:20,160 --> 00:03:21,370
That's the handoff.

74
00:03:22,740 --> 00:03:24,870
Now change one thing at a time.

75
00:03:25,180 --> 00:03:29,010
Add sideways drift, and a support
feature can miss its receiving surface.

76
00:03:30,220 --> 00:03:33,490
Add tilt, and one side can
meet the arms before the other,

77
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:35,230
changing how the load is shared.

78
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:38,100
Keep too much downward speed,

79
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:40,490
and a gentle handoff becomes an impact.

80
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:43,340
These are simple examples,

81
00:03:43,380 --> 00:03:45,270
but they explain the real problem.

82
00:03:45,580 --> 00:03:48,770
Position, rotation and
speed have to cooperate.

83
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:52,790
The engines can't leave the tower to
absorb whatever motion happens to remain.

84
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:55,730
And because the receiving machine
is part of the launch site,

85
00:03:55,900 --> 00:03:59,120
a serious failure could damage the
equipment needed for the next flight

86
00:03:59,160 --> 00:04:00,060
too.

87
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:04,410
That makes deciding when to attempt
the catch part of the engineering.

88
00:04:04,680 --> 00:04:06,860
A vehicle problem, an engine problem,

89
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:10,670
or a problem on the ground can remove
the conditions for a safe approach.

90
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:14,540
Flight 6 already showed that tower
health checks could call off a booster

91
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:15,360
catch.

92
00:04:15,400 --> 00:04:18,970
Alternatives depend on the mission
plan and when the problem is detected.

93
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:22,470
They aren't a guaranteed escape
once the rocket is beside the arms.

94
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:26,140
Protecting the receiving machinery
matters as much as getting a dramatic

95
00:04:26,180 --> 00:04:27,910
picture of a rocket coming home.

96
00:04:29,460 --> 00:04:33,140
Moving recovery machinery onto the
ground keeps some of that machinery off

97
00:04:33,180 --> 00:04:34,250
the flying vehicle.

98
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:38,800
Recovering both stages also creates the
possibility of using the same expensive

99
00:04:38,840 --> 00:04:39,790
hardware again.

100
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:43,550
That's the prize behind this
ridiculous-looking maneuver.

101
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:47,230
But bringing a spacecraft home
is the beginning of another job:

102
00:04:47,480 --> 00:04:48,710
checking the heat shield,

103
00:04:48,760 --> 00:04:50,070
engines and structure,

104
00:04:50,280 --> 00:04:51,990
then proving it can fly again.

105
00:04:52,240 --> 00:04:52,900
Catch it.

106
00:04:52,940 --> 00:04:53,820
Inspect it.

107
00:04:53,860 --> 00:04:54,770
Fly it again.

108
00:04:54,880 --> 00:04:55,780
Repeat.

109
00:04:56,560 --> 00:04:58,790
The goal is a system that keeps working,

110
00:04:58,860 --> 00:05:01,470
rather than a spectacular
trick performed once.

111
00:05:01,860 --> 00:05:05,700
Every successful return moves that
idea closer to a working transportation

112
00:05:05,740 --> 00:05:06,640
system.

113
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:10,510
So watch the final seconds differently.

114
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:12,930
The heat shield has done its job.

115
00:05:13,430 --> 00:05:15,220
The flaps have controlled the descent.

116
00:05:15,840 --> 00:05:18,070
The engines have turned
and slowed the ship.

117
00:05:18,980 --> 00:05:21,730
Now the supports meet, and
the tower takes the load.

118
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:26,990
That's the sequence SpaceX wants
to complete with both stages.

119
00:05:27,560 --> 00:05:29,510
The first catch brought the booster home.

120
00:05:30,220 --> 00:05:33,270
The next challenge is bringing home
the spacecraft that went to orbit—

121
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:35,670
and eventually sending it back up again.
